# The commit-msg hook must keep LF endings, on every platform.
#
# Git for Windows defaults to core.autocrlf=true, which rewrites LF to CRLF on
# checkout. A shell script survives that everywhere except its first line: the
# shebang becomes "#!/bin/sh\r", and the kernel -- or Git's own sh on Windows --
# looks for an interpreter whose name ends in a carriage return and does not
# find one. The hook then fails with "bad interpreter", which on a commit-msg
# hook reads as the gate being broken rather than the file being mangled.
#
# Scoped to the hooks directory on purpose. A blanket rule here would restate
# line endings for every tracked file, including vendored third-party trees,
# and produce a checkout-wide diff nobody asked for.
claude/githooks/* text eol=lf
